From: omarchy-m5
Subject: [PATCH] payload: carry the stage 1 chainload target inside the image

The usual stage 1 is m1n1.bin with a chainload= variable appended. On the
J714s (T6050, mBoot-20457.60.65.0.1) both objects that had something
appended boot-looped when enrolled: a few resets within about a minute, with
none of their code reaching USB. Their sizes were not multiples of 16 KiB.
The plain m1n1.bin that boots is, and so are the other raw objects known to
start on this model; Aurora's stage 1 likewise has its configuration filled
in place so that the image keeps its length and its STACKBOT tail.

So the enrolled image is a plain m1n1.bin again and knows its target itself:
src/stage1_chainload.h, written by scripts/prepare-m1n1-gpu.py, defines
STAGE1_CHAINLOAD as "<partition uuid>;<path>". An appended chainload=
variable still wins, for images that are chainloaded rather than enrolled.
When the target cannot be read, payload_run() fails as before and the
loader falls back to its proxy.

---
diff --git a/src/payload.c b/src/payload.c
index 5b1547f..ac95033 100644
--- a/src/payload.c
+++ b/src/payload.c
@@ -13,6 +13,7 @@
 #include "kboot.h"
 #include "mitigations.h"
 #include "smp.h"
+#include "stage1_chainload.h"
 #include "utils.h"
 
 #include "libfdt/libfdt.h"
@@ -309,6 +310,16 @@ int payload_run(void)
     while (p)
         p = load_one_payload(p, 0);
 
+    if (!chainload_spec) {
+        // Keep the target inside the image, preserving its aligned size and
+        // STACKBOT tail. Both unaligned enrolled candidates boot-looped
+        // before USB appeared; alignment versus other native-startup causes
+        // has not yet been isolated.
+        static char builtin[] = STAGE1_CHAINLOAD;
+        printf("Built-in chainload target: %s\n", builtin);
+        chainload_spec = builtin;
+    }
+
     if (chainload_spec) {
         return chainload_load(chainload_spec, chosen, chosen_cnt);
     }
